dflash2: keep the drafter's residual stream inside fp16 range (Turing / sm75).

The DFlash2 drafter is trained in bf16. Its residual stream grows past 6e4 by the end of
layer 1 (measured 58,592 at TP=1 on a real request) and keeps growing, past fp16's 65,504.
Turing has no fast bf16, so there the whole stack runs --dtype float16 and the drafter
inherits it: layer 2's input norm turns inf into NaN and every later layer is NaN. The
drafts are then noise: 1 accepted of 434 drafted with lookup off, ~2% overall with lookup
on, and DFlash2 decoded slower than SPEC=off (~40 vs 55 tok/s, 2x RTX 2080 Ti, TP=2).

Every norm in the drafter is a pre-norm RMSNorm, which is invariant to a common scale on
its input, so the residual is carried at 1/32 scale: the first layer scales the residual
it starts from, and each layer scales its attention and MLP outputs before the (linear)
grouped conv adds them in. The normed activations and the final norm's output are
unchanged; the peak residual becomes ~4.5e3 (about 14x headroom). After: 50% draft
acceptance (4.5 tokens/step), 192 tok/s on code and 139 tok/s on prose single-stream,
greedy output byte-identical to SPEC=off.

On by default only for float16; bf16 keeps scale 1 and is untouched.
VLLM_DFLASH2_RESIDUAL_SCALE overrides it (1 disables).

Kind: fix
What: keep the DFlash2 drafter's residual stream inside fp16 range (Turing/sm75 runs fp16; without it every draft is NaN)
Upstream: none yet
Cut-against: 0.33.0
Retires-when: upstream scales or otherwise bounds the drafter residual for fp16

